\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Why the spatial null is a calibrated screen and not an exchangeable rank test. \emph  {(a)}\nobreakspace  {}add-one rank of the star among its 512{} controls, for the survey and for the held-out windows carrying no attributed line: both lie above the uniform diagonal. The dotted curve is the \emph  {pseudo-stars}, control positions at the inner edge of the annulus ranked against the other controls, which contain no star and are displaced just as far (\S  \ref  {sec:radial}), so the displacement is instrumental. \emph  {(b)}\nobreakspace  {}the control statistic itself against radius from the pointing centre, in units of each window's own noise scale. It is lowest at mid-annulus and rises towards both edges; the shaded strip inside $0.14\,\theta _{\rm  PB}$ holds no control probe at all, and that is where the star sits (median 0.0035$\,\theta _{\rm  PB}$).}}{14}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rankcdf}{{4}{14}{Why the spatial null is a calibrated screen and not an exchangeable rank test. \emph {(a)}~add-one rank of the star among its \NCtrl {} controls, for the survey and for the held-out windows carrying no attributed line: both lie above the uniform diagonal. The dotted curve is the \emph {pseudo-stars}, control positions at the inner edge of the annulus ranked against the other controls, which contain no star and are displaced just as far (\S \ref {sec:radial}), so the displacement is instrumental. \emph {(b)}~the control statistic itself against radius from the pointing centre, in units of each window's own noise scale. It is lowest at mid-annulus and rises towards both edges; the shaded strip inside $\RadRIn \,\theta _{\rm PB}$ holds no control probe at all, and that is where the star sits (median \RadUStarMed $\,\theta _{\rm PB}$)}{figure.4}{}}
