\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The drift-rate ceiling as a physical selection function. Maximum star--planet relative acceleration, edge-on and maximised over orbital phase, as the carrier drift rate it induces (Hz\,s$^{-1}$ per GHz of carrier frequency; equivalently $\dot  \nu /\nu =\dot  v/c$), against orbital period, for host-star masses 0.09--1.8\,$M_{\odot }$ (lines) and the known planets of the planet-hosting targets (points). Shaded band: the survey's drift-search ceiling, above which a transmitter lies outside the searched grid. Only TRAPPIST-1\,b reaches it (\S  \ref  {sec:discussion}).}}{24}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{8}{24}{The drift-rate ceiling as a physical selection function. Maximum star--planet relative acceleration, edge-on and maximised over orbital phase, as the carrier drift rate it induces (Hz\,s$^{-1}$ per GHz of carrier frequency; equivalently $\dot \nu /\nu =\dot v/c$), against orbital period, for host-star masses 0.09--1.8\,$M_{\odot }$ (lines) and the known planets of the planet-hosting targets (points). Shaded band: the survey's drift-search ceiling, above which a transmitter lies outside the searched grid. Only TRAPPIST-1\,b reaches it (\S \ref {sec:discussion})}{figure.8}{}}
